---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

const project = projects.find(
  (project) => project.link === "/work/prompt-guide",
);

if (!project) {
  throw new Error("Project not found");
}
---

<Layout>
  <main>
    <article class="project-page">
      <a class="back-link" href="/">← Back to portfolio</a>

      <header class="project-header">
        <p class="project-label">
          Instructional Guide · Generative AI Tools · Individual Project
        </p>
        <h1>{project?.title}</h1>

        <p class="project-intro">
          {project?.description}
        </p>
        <div class="project-resources">
          <a
            class="button"
            href="/files/prompt-guide-final.pdf"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            View Final Deliverable
          </a>
          <a
            class="button button-secondary"
            href="/files/prompt-guide-audience-analysis.pdf"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            View Audience Analysis
          </a>
          <a
            class="button button-secondary"
            href="/files/prompt-guide-project-narrative.pdf"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            View Project Narrative
          </a>         
        </div>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>The Challenge</h2>
        <p>
          Prompt engineering is quickly becoming an important skill for
          non-technical professionals, but much of the existing documentation is
          overly technical, informal, or inconsistent.
        </p>
        <p>
          My challenge was to create clear, practical guidance that helps
          non-technical users improve AI output by writing more effective
          prompts.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>My Role</h2>
        <p>
          This was an individual project that I researched, wrote, edited, and
          designed.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Audience</h2>
        <p>
          The primary audience for this guide was non-developer knowledge
          workers who use generative AI tools but lack experience in programming
          or machine learning. The audience analysis identified several common
          needs and concerns.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Limited or no understanding of how LLMs work</li>
          <li>
            A desire for immediate, practical guidance rather than technical
            explanations
          </li>
          <li>Frustration with inconsistent or low-quality AI outputs</li>
          <li>
            Concerns about using AI responsibly and appropriately in
            professional contexts
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Approach</h2>
        <p>
          Using a writer-centered analysis worksheet, I identified the
          professional roles of my primary, secondary, tertiary, and gatekeeper
          readers. I then used a reader analysis worksheet to document each
          group’s needs, values, attitudes, and the contexts in which they would
          use the deliverable. I defined the content scope by outlining the
          desired outcomes of the guide for primary readers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Result</h2>
        <p>
          The final deliverable provides jargon-free, practical instruction
          rather than technical theory. It includes clear, step-by-step
          techniques that can be applied immediately, a simple framework for
          constructing effective prompts, and examples that mirror real
          workplace tasks.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
